1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,881
We're pleased to welcome Dr Martin Merry whether of the Antarctic Centre in Christchurch,

2
00:00:06,881 --> 00:00:11,514
New Zealand who has come along to talk to us today about the role of the Centre

3
00:00:11,514 --> 00:00:13,360
and the Antarctic Treaty.

4
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:17,502
Now my first question is about the choice of location for the centre.

5
00:00:17,502 --> 00:00:18,674
Why Christchurch?

6
00:00:18,674 --> 00:00:20,800
Was it because of the climate?

7
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:25,489
Well actually New Zealand is the second closest country to Antarctica

8
00:00:25,489 --> 00:00:28,900
and Christchurch is often used on Antarctic expeditions.

9
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:29,289
Right,

10
00:00:29,289 --> 00:00:32,010
so it's because of where we are...

11
00:00:32,010 --> 00:00:33,953
coupled with our historical role.

12
00:00:33,953 --> 00:00:37,840
So tell us-what is the main purpose of the centre?

13
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:38,237
Well...

14
00:00:38,237 --> 00:00:40,220
we have two complementary roles.

15
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:44,187
One is as a scientific base for expeditions and research

16
00:00:44,187 --> 00:00:47,360
and the other is as an information centre.

17
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:50,950
Tell us something about the role as a scientific base.

18
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:56,307
We're able to provide information about what scientists should take with them to the

19
00:00:56,307 --> 00:01:01,664
South Pole-for example, the centre contains a clothing warehouse where expeditions are supplied with

20
00:01:01,664 --> 00:01:03,960
suitable clothing for the extreme conditions.

21
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:07,180
I suppose you need a bit more than your normal winter coat!

22
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:12,400
Yes, exactly and then there's also the specialist library and mapping services.

23
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:12,896
Right.

24
00:01:12,896 --> 00:01:17,360
And which countries are actually located at the centre?

25
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:17,704
Well...

26
00:01:17,704 --> 00:01:22,520
the centre houses research programmes for New Zealand, for The United States as well

27
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:23,552
as for Italy...

28
00:01:23,552 --> 00:01:27,680
there's even a US post office at the American airforce base here.

29
00:01:27,680 --> 00:01:28,141
Really?

30
00:01:28,141 --> 00:01:31,370
And what does the visitor's centre offer?

31
00:01:31,370 --> 00:01:37,237
Well, since very few people will ever experience the Antarctic first hand, the visitors'

32
00:01:37,237 --> 00:01:40,590
centre aims to recreate the atmosphere of Antarctica.

33
00:01:40,590 --> 00:01:44,988
There's a mock camp site where you can see inside an Antarctic tent

34
00:01:44,988 --> 00:01:46,680
and imagine yourself sleeping there.

35
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:52,109
And the centre also acts as a showcase for the unique international co-operation which

36
00:01:52,109 --> 00:01:53,660
exists in Antarctica today.

37
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:56,592
What is it actually like at the South Pole?

38
00:01:56,592 --> 00:01:59,850
I know you've been there on a number of occasions.

39
00:01:59,850 --> 00:02:05,285
Yes, I have and each time I'm struck by the awesome beauty of the

40
00:02:05,285 --> 00:02:05,673
place.

41
00:02:05,673 --> 00:02:10,720
It's magnificent but you can really only visit it in the summer months.

42
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:12,350
October to March.

43
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:12,718
Yes,

44
00:02:12,718 --> 00:02:16,765
because it's completely dark for four months of the year (pause) ...

45
00:02:16,765 --> 00:02:21,180
and in addition it has to be the coldest place on earth.

46
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:23,851
Colder than the North Pole?

47
00:02:23,851 --> 00:02:24,920
Why's that?

48
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:26,763
Well, unlike the North Pole,

49
00:02:26,763 --> 00:02:31,925
which is actually a frozen sea, Antarctica is a land mass shaped like a

50
00:02:31,925 --> 00:02:37,087
dome, with the result that the winds blow down the slopes at speeds of

51
00:02:37,087 --> 00:02:41,880
up to 150 km an hour and that's what makes it so cold.

52
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:46,840
And one other interesting thing is that Antarctica is the driest continent on earth,

53
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:47,194
surprisingly,

54
00:02:47,194 --> 00:02:51,800
and so you have to drink large amounts of water when you're there.

55
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:54,000
How old is Antarctica?

56
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,111
We're pretty sure it was part of a larger land mass

57
00:02:57,111 --> 00:03:01,070
but it broke away from the rest of the continent 170 million years ago.

58
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:03,000
How can you be certain of this?

59
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,663
...because fossils and rocks have been discovered in Antarctica which are the same as

60
00:03:07,663 --> 00:03:10,660
those found in places such as Africa and Australia.

61
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:11,098
Amazing...

62
00:03:11,098 --> 00:03:15,040
To think that it was once attached to Africa...

63
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:19,097
Now let's just have a look at the Antarctic Treaty.

64
00:03:19,097 --> 00:03:23,560
How far back does the idea of an international treaty go?

65
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:27,117
Well, as far back as the 19th century,

66
00:03:27,117 --> 00:03:30,230
when eleven nations organised an international event.

67
00:03:30,230 --> 00:03:32,000
When was that exactly?

68
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:32,875
In 1870.

69
00:03:32,875 --> 00:03:36,376
And it was called the Polar Research Meeting.

70
00:03:36,376 --> 00:03:42,502
And then, not long after that, they organised something called the First International Polar

71
00:03:42,502 --> 00:03:42,940
Year.

72
00:03:42,940 --> 00:03:45,760
And that took place when exactly?

73
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:49,316
Over two years from 1882 to 1883.

74
00:03:49,316 --> 00:03:56,428
But it wasn't until the 1950s that the idea of an international treaty was

75
00:03:56,428 --> 00:03:56,936
proposed.

76
00:03:56,936 --> 00:04:01,000
And in 1959 the Treaty was actually signed.

77
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,870
What do you see as the main achievements of the treaty?

78
00:04:04,870 --> 00:04:10,050
Well, firstly it means that the continent is reserved for peaceful use.

79
00:04:10,050 --> 00:04:12,950
That's Article 1, isn't it?

80
00:04:12,950 --> 00:04:13,830
Yes...

81
00:04:13,830 --> 00:04:17,290
That's important since the territory belongs to everyone.

82
00:04:17,290 --> 00:04:20,735
Yes but not as important as Article 5,

83
00:04:20,735 --> 00:04:24,180
which prohibits any nuclear explosions or waste disposal.

84
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:25,065
Which is marvellous.

85
00:04:25,065 --> 00:04:28,015
Well, I'm afraid we're going to have to stop there

86
00:04:28,015 --> 00:04:30,375
because I'm afraid we've run out of time.

87
00:04:30,375 --> 00:04:33,915
Thanks for coming along today and telling us all about the centre

88
00:04:33,915 --> 00:04:34,800
and its work.
